## Todo List for AI Agent — Fix Chat Response Quality (Ollama Qwen 4B)

### 1. Audit and rewrite the system prompt
- [ ] Define agent role, scope, and persona in one clear paragraph (what it is, what it is NOT — e.g. not a general chatbot, only a stock/alert assistant)
- [ ] Specify the exact output format allowed per use case (plain text vs markdown vs JSON) — never leave format open-ended
- [ ] Write an explicit banned-phrases list: "I'd be happy to help", "Let's assume", "Here's an example", "Please provide...", "Feel free to...", etc.
- [ ] Add a rule: no meta-commentary about the AI's own process ("I don't see any data provided" type narration) — either ask one short clarifying question or fail silently to a fallback message
- [ ] Add 3-5 few-shot examples covering: (a) normal case with full data, (b) missing/partial data case, (c) ambiguous request case, (d) malformed input case — show ideal output for each
- [ ] Add explicit length constraints in the prompt itself ("respond in under X words/tokens unless asked for detail")
- [ ] Version the system prompt (e.g. `prompts/system_v1.md`) so changes are trackable and revertible

### 2. Add a "missing/invalid context" guard in application code
- [ ] Write a validation function that checks required fields (symbol, price, thresholds, etc.) exist and are well-formed before the model is ever called
- [ ] Define pre-written fallback messages for each known failure mode (no data, partial data, invalid symbol, stale data) instead of letting the LLM improvise
- [ ] Log every time the guard triggers a fallback, with the reason, for later analysis of how often this happens in production
- [ ] Add a unit test for each fallback path to ensure it never silently breaks

### 3. Enforce a single, strict output format
- [ ] Decide format per endpoint/use case (e.g. chat UI = markdown-lite, alert payloads = strict JSON) and document it
- [ ] For structured responses, use Ollama's `format: "json"` mode with a defined JSON schema rather than prompting "respond in JSON"
- [ ] Add schema validation (e.g. via `pydantic` or `zod`) on the model's JSON output before it's used downstream; reject and retry once if invalid
- [ ] For text responses, strip any stray markdown artifacts (code fences, headers, asterisks) that don't belong in the target format
- [ ] Reject/re-prompt if the model mixes formats (e.g. JSON inside prose) rather than trying to parse it

### 4. Build a post-processing/normalization layer
- [ ] Strip common preamble/postamble patterns via regex or a lightweight rules engine (before parsing/rendering)
- [ ] Truncate to a max character/token count with graceful sentence-boundary cutoff, not mid-word
- [ ] Deduplicate repeated sentences/phrases if they appear (common small-model failure mode)
- [ ] Consider an optional lightweight "formatter" LLM pass (cheap, low temperature) that rewrites raw output into final style — only if rules-based cleanup proves insufficient
- [ ] Add a final sanity check: if cleaned output is empty or under N characters, fall back to a safe default message

### 5. Tune generation parameters
- [ ] Set `temperature` to 0.2–0.4 range and A/B test against current setting
- [ ] Set `num_predict` (max tokens) to a sane cap based on expected use case (e.g. 150-300 for chat, more for reports)
- [ ] Test `repeat_penalty` and `top_p` adjustments if repetition or rambling persists after temperature fix
- [ ] Record each parameter combination tested and its qualitative outcome in a short changelog

### 6. Build a regression/eval test suite
- [ ] Create 15-20 representative test prompts covering: full data, missing data, partial data, ambiguous phrasing, malformed JSON input, and edge-case tickers/values
- [ ] Store expected "good" output characteristics per test (not necessarily exact text, but format + tone + length rules)
- [ ] Automate running this suite after every prompt/config/code change, and diff outputs against the last known-good baseline
- [ ] Add a simple scoring rubric (format correctness, tone correctness, no filler phrases, length within bounds) to quantify "better/worse" instead of relying on eyeballing

### 7. Benchmark model size/capability
- [ ] Run the full eval suite against Qwen 7B/14B and Llama 3.1 8B (or similar) using the same prompt and config
- [ ] Compare latency, resource cost, and quality score across models
- [ ] Report a clear recommendation: stay on 4B with more scaffolding, upgrade model size, or fine-tune 4B on domain examples
- [ ] If fine-tuning is chosen, curate a dataset of 50-100+ high-quality input/output pairs from the eval suite and real usage logs

### 8. Documentation and guardrails for future changes
- [ ] Document final system prompt, output schema, generation params, and post-processing rules in the repo (`docs/agent-behavior.md`)
- [ ] Note any known limitations of the current model size so future contributors don't "fix" the same issue blindly
- [ ] Set up an alert/log if fallback messages trigger above a certain rate in production (signals prompt or validation drift)